
  <sn><md>### Soil Acidity

**Definition:**
Soil acidity is the condition where soil pH is **below 7.0**, caused by the accumulation of **H⁺ and Al³⁺ ions** in the soil solution and on exchange sites. Soils with pH below **5.5** are considered strongly acidic.

**Causes:**
- **Heavy rainfall** — leaches base cations (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺), leaving H⁺ behind
- **Acid rain** — H₂SO₄ and HNO₃ from pollution
- **Ammonium fertilizers** — nitrification releases H⁺:
$$
\text{NH}_4^+ + 2\text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{NO}_3^- + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O}
$$
- **Organic matter decomposition** — releases organic acids and CO₂
- **Plant root activity** — roots release H⁺ to absorb cations
- **Acid mine drainage** — releases H⁺ and heavy metals
- **Parent material** — acidic rocks (granite, sandstone)
</md></sn>
