<sn><md>### Types of Soil Acidity:

| Type | Description |
|---|---|
| **Active acidity** | H⁺ in soil solution, measured by pH |
| **Exchange acidity** | H⁺ and Al³⁺ on exchange sites |
| **Residual acidity** | H⁺ bound in organic/clay structures |
| **Potential acidity** | Exchange + residual (total reserve) |

**Role of Al³⁺:**
In acid soils (pH &lt; 5.5), **Al³⁺** becomes soluble and dominant on exchange sites. Its hydrolysis releases H⁺:
$$
\text{Al}^{3+} + 3\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{Al(OH)}_3 + 3\text{H}^+
$$
Al³⁺ is the **main toxic factor** in acid soils.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Effects on Soil
- **Nutrient deficiency** — P fixed by Fe/Al; Ca, Mg, K leached
- **Al³⁺ and Mn²⁺ toxicity** — damage plant roots
- **Reduced microbial activity** — N fixation and decomposition slow
- **Poor structure** — low base saturation weakens aggregates

**Effects on Crops:**
- Stunted root growth
- Poor nutrient uptake
- Yellowing (chlorosis)
- Reduced yield
</md></sn><sn><md>### Remediation:

- **Liming** — CaCO₃ or Ca(OH)₂ neutralizes acidity:
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{CaCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{CO}_2
$$
- **Organic matter** — buffers pH
- **Balanced fertilization** — avoid excess ammonium fertilizers
- **Acid-tolerant crops** — tea, potato, pineapple

**Example:**
In **Assam tea gardens**, heavy rainfall and ammonium fertilizer use have made soils acidic (pH &lt; 5.0). Regular liming is needed to maintain productivity.

**Conclusion:**
Soil acidity is the lowering of pH below 7.0 due to **H⁺ and Al³⁺ accumulation**. It causes nutrient deficiency and Al toxicity, reducing crop yield. It is managed by **liming, organic matter addition, and balanced fertilization**.</md></sn>